aroff="50">1.8</td><td align="char" valign="top" char="." charoff="50">1.9</td><td align="char" valign="top" char="." charoff="50">2.1</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">&#x02003;Red meat (servings&#x02009;week<sup>&#x02212;1</sup>)</td><td align="char" valign="top" char="." charoff="50">2.9</td><td align="char" valign="top" char="." charoff="50">3.0</td><td align="char" valign="top" char="." charoff="50">3.0</td><td align="char" valign="top" char="." charoff="50">3.1</td><td align="char" valign="top" char="." charoff="50">3.1</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tbfnote1"><label>a</label><p>Whole grains include hard whole grain rye bread, soft whole grain bread, porridge, and cold breakfast cereals.</p></fn><fn id="tbfnote2"><label>b</label><p>Nutrients adjusted to the rounded mean energy intake (1350&#x02009;kcal&#x02009;day<sup>&#x02212;1</sup>) in the cohort at baseline.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="tbl2" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p content-type="table-title">RR and 95&#x00025; CI of colorectal cancer according to consumption of whole grains</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" border="1" width="85%"><colgroup><col align="left"/><col align="center"/><col align="center"/><col align="center"/><col align="center"/><col align="center"/><col align="center"/></colgroup><thead valign="bottom"><tr><th align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">&#x000a0;</th><th colspan="5" align="center" valign="top" charoff="50"><bold>Categories of whole grain consumption (servings&#x02009;day<sup>&#x02212;1</sup>)</bold><hr/></th><th align="center" valign="top" charoff="50">&#x000a0;</th></tr><tr><th align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">&#x000a0;</th><th align="center" valign="top" charoff="50"><bold>&#x0003c;1.5</bold></th><th align="center" valign="top" charoff="50"><bold>1.5&#x02013;2.4</bold></th><th align="center" valign="top" charoff="50"><bold>2.5&#x02013;3.4</bold></th><th align="cent