<tr><td align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">&#x02003;&#x02003;Multivariate RR (95&#x00025; CI)<sup>a</sup></td><td align="center" valign="top" charoff="50">1.00</td><td align="center" valign="top" charoff="50">1.21 (0.81&#x02013;1.81)</td><td align="center" valign="top" charoff="50">1.02 (0.66&#x02013;1.58)</td><td align="center" valign="top" charoff="50">1.11 (0.69&#x02013;1.79)</td><td align="center" valign="top" charoff="50">1.07 (0.62&#x02013;1.82)</td><td align="center" valign="top" charoff="50">0.99</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tbfnote3"><label>a</label><p>Multivariate RRs were adjusted for age, body mass index (quartiles), education (less than high school, high school, or university), total energy intake (continuous), and quartiles of intakes of saturated fat, calcium, red meat, fruits, and vegetables.</p></fn><fn id="tbfnote4"><label>b</label><p>Six cases diagnosed with both colon and rectal cancer are excluded from subsite-specific analysis. RR=rate ratio; CI=confidence interval.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-wrap></article>